course: medicine
lesson: 5
title: Nosebleeds and bumps on the head
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: The front of your nose is lined with lots of tiny blood vessels close to the surface, so dry air, picking or a knock can make them bleed.
explain: To stop a nosebleed, sit down, lean forward and pinch the soft part of the nose for 10 to 15 minutes; tipping your head back sends the blood down your throat.
explain: Your brain floats inside your skull, and a hard knock, like a tackle in rugby, can shake it: that is concussion, and you don't have to be knocked out to have it.
explain: Grandma's old rule says stay awake after a bump, but doctors now say sleeping is fine once a grown-up has checked you and can wake you easily.
explain: A bike helmet that cracks in a crash did its job: it broke so your head didn't.
fact: To stop a nosebleed, sit leaning forward and pinch the soft part of the nose for 10 to 15 minutes, breathing through the mouth.
fact: Tipping the head back is wrong, because blood runs down the throat and can make you sick.
fact: Frequent nosebleeds in children usually come from one fragile vessel, picking or dry air, such as heated air in winter, and are rarely serious; most children grow out of them.
fact: A bump on the head swells because the scalp has many blood vessels that leak under the skin; the size of the bump does not show how serious the injury is.
fact: Concussion is a brain injury from the brain being shaken; it can cause headache, dizziness, sickness, confusion and memory gaps.
fact: You can have concussion without being knocked out.
fact: After concussion, a day or two of quiet rest is followed by a slow return to school, then to sport over at least two to three weeks.
fact: Doctors now say it is fine to sleep after a mild head bump, as long as a grown-up has checked you and can wake you easily.
fact: Warning signs after a head injury include being hard to wake, being sick again and again, a worsening headache, confusion, a fit, or clear fluid from the nose or ears.
fact: Anyone knocked out, even briefly, should be checked by a doctor; hospitals sometimes do a CT scan, a special 3D X-ray, of the head.
fact: A bike helmet is built to crack and crush to soak up the force, and it should be replaced after any big crash.
fact: A crooked nose is checked after a few days, when the swelling has gone down.
fact: A knocked-out adult tooth can be rinsed briefly and pushed back or kept in milk on the way to a dentist; a baby tooth is not put back, and a chipped or knocked-out tooth should be seen by a dentist the same day.
exercise: Your nose starts bleeding in class. What should you do? | Sit, lean forward and pinch the soft part of the nose | Tip your head right back and look at the ceiling | Lie flat on the floor with your arms by your sides and wait | Blow your nose hard to clear it
exercise: How long should you pinch a bleeding nose? | About 10 to 15 minutes without letting go | About 10 seconds, then check it | Until the lesson ends, however long that is | Just once quickly, then let go
exercise: Why are frequent nosebleeds in children usually not serious? | They usually come from one fragile vessel or dry air | Children have extra blood that needs to come out somewhere | They mean the nose is growing | Nose blood is not real blood, just water
exercise: Why does a bump on the head swell up like an egg? | Scalp blood vessels leak under the skin | The skull grows a small new bone there | Air gets trapped under the hair and puffs up | The brain pushes out against the bump
exercise: What is concussion? | A brain injury from the brain being shaken | A broken nose from a fall | A bruise on the forehead only, nothing inside | A kind of headache from screens
exercise: Do you have to be knocked out to have concussion? | No, many people with concussion stay awake | Yes, always, otherwise it isn't concussion | Yes, for at least an hour | No, concussion only happens while you sleep
exercise: Grandma says don't sleep after a head bump. What do doctors say now? | Sleep is fine if a grown-up has checked you and can wake you | Stay wide awake for three days and nights | Sleep standing up so the bump drains | Sleep only with the light on
exercise: Your friend was knocked out for a moment but got up fine. What next? | He should be checked by a doctor | Nothing, he is clearly fine if he got up | He should finish the game first and see | He should sleep it off at his desk in class
exercise: Why should you replace a helmet that cracked in a crash? | It did its job and won't protect as well again | Cracked helmets get too heavy to wear safely on your head | It will fall apart the next time it rains | Cracks make helmets illegal to ride in
exercise: Why no screens or homework for a bit after concussion? | The brain needs a short rest to recover | Screens on their own give you a second concussion | Homework causes more bumps on the head from stress | Teachers just want a holiday from marking it
exercise: When is a crooked nose properly checked? | After a few days, when the swelling goes down | Straight away, by pushing it back yourself | Only after a whole year has gone by and it has set | Never, because noses always fix themselves
exercise: You chipped a front tooth. What should happen? | Go to the dentist the same day | Glue the chip back on at home tonight | Wait until the tooth falls out by itself | Brush it harder so the chip smooths over
exercise: Which is a warning sign after a head bump? | Being sick again and again | A small lump that is a bit sore | Feeling a bit sad about falling | Wanting a snack and a drink of juice
exercise: Which tooth should NOT be pushed back in after it is knocked out? | A baby tooth | An adult front tooth | Any tooth in a grown-up | A tooth kept in milk
exercise: How long is the slow return to sport after concussion? | At least two to three weeks | The very next day, if you feel fine | One hour, after a drink of water | Never again, for the rest of your life
exercise: What scan might a hospital do after a bad head knock? | A CT scan, a special 3D X-ray | A hearing test in a quiet room | An eye chart with letters | A blood pressure cuff on the arm
test: nose pouring blood, mate says chuck ur head back. yeah? | No, lean forward so blood doesn't run down your throat | Yes, head back is the only way to stop it | Yes, and lie down flat too | No, hang upside down off a chair till it stops
test: Tom got a bang in rugby and feels dizzy and sick but wasn't knocked out. Can he keep playing? | No, it may be concussion; stop and get checked | Yes, no knock-out means no concussion | Yes, after a drink and a sit down | No, but he can go back on for the second half after a rest
test: Which is a reason to get urgent help after a head injury? | Clear fluid coming from the nose or ears | A bump about the size of a grape | Being a bit tired after dinner that evening | Saying the bump hurts when touched
test: Lucy's helmet cracked in her bike crash. Her brother says it was a rubbish helmet. Right? | No, a helmet cracking means it absorbed the force | Yes, good helmets never crack, however hard you hit | Yes, it should be glued and reused | No, helmets don't actually do anything
test: In a cartoon, the cat gets bonked on the head, sees stars and is fine a second later. What's wrong? | A hard knock can cause concussion that needs rest and checking | Nothing, head bumps are always harmless | Cats can't get bumps, only people can | Seeing stars just means your brain has already healed itself
test: Why do some kids get nosebleeds more in winter? | Dry heated air dries the inside of the nose | Cold weather makes the blood thinner and runnier | Snow gets up into the nose | They eat more sweets in winter and get hot
test: Max's nose looks bent after a football hit. He wants to push it straight himself. Good idea? | No, a doctor should check it once the swelling goes down | Yes, push it hard now before it sets | Yes, before school so nobody notices | No, bend it the other way first to balance it out
test: Dad says after concussion you lie in the dark doing nothing for a month. Right? | No, gentle activity after the first days helps recovery | Yes, a whole month of darkness and silence | Yes, and no food either | No, play rugby the same day to shake it off
test: What would happen if you tipped your head back during a nosebleed? | Blood could run down your throat and make you sick | The bleeding would stop in a second | Your nose would grow a bit | Nothing at all happens either way
test: A big bump means a bad injury and no bump means you're fine. True? | No, the size of the bump doesn't tell you how serious it is | Yes, bumps show exactly how bad it is | Yes, no bump means no injury at all | No, bumps only ever come from small knocks
test: Is it fine to go to sleep after a mild head bump? | Yes, if a grown-up has checked you and can wake you | No, never sleep that day, whatever happens | Only if you sleep sitting up in a chair | Only if someone shouts at you every minute all night
exam: Identify the recommended posture for the management of a nosebleed. | Seated, leaning forward, with the soft part of the nose compressed | Supine, with the head tilted backwards | Standing, with the head inverted below the heart | Seated, with the nose blown forcefully and repeatedly
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises concussion? | A cerebral injury which may occur without loss of consciousness | An injury occurring only upon loss of consciousness | A fracture invariably affecting the nasal bones | A disorder of the head caused exclusively by prolonged use of screens
exam: Select the response consistent with current advice concerning sleep following a minor injury to the head. | Sleep is permissible once a responsible adult has examined the child | Sleep is to be prohibited for three days | Sleep is to be undertaken only in a seated posture | Sleep is permissible only with the child woken by an adult every minute
exam: It is given that a bicycle helmet has cracked in a collision. State the appropriate course of action. | The helmet is to be replaced, having absorbed the force | The helmet is to be repaired with adhesive and reused thereafter | The helmet is to be retained, being undamaged within | The helmet is to be worn reversed
